 / `show [<rev>]`; `.githooks/post-commit` writes it after every commit (never fails a commit). In Lua: `require('cartograph.provenance').attribute(repo, range)` (the shared line-level attribution), `.ledger_row`, `.write_note`, `.read_note`. Notes travel only when pushed (`git push origin refs/notes/cartograph`). ★ The ledger only TIGHTENS: a note claiming a decision accepted answers nothing (pinned). An entry explains ONLY the commit its change landed in (CART-1195): made no later than the commit, not already in its parent, and — when older than the parent commit — none of it in the parent; before, 22 of 135 entries were credited to several commits. CART-1180 |\n| May this record LEAVE THE MACHINE? (a ledger note, a token, an invocation — does it carry an untracked file's bytes, a `.env`?) | `require('cartograph.sensitive')` — `classify(root, rels)` → `{ [rel] = why }`: DERIVED, a file git tracks is public, untracked/ignored is sensitive, a world with no repo is all sensitive; user `config.at(<abs>, 'sensitive') = true/false` overrides (never the tree). `reference(v)` = what travels in a sensitive value's place (`{ ref = 'sha256:…', sensitive = true }`). `leaks(record, images)` = the EGRESS check, independent of the redaction: any line (≥ 12 chars) of a sensitive file's images anywhere in the record's strings → `{ file, why }`, the bytes never named. Wired: every journal entry now records its INVOCATION (`{ verb, args, where }`, CART-1191 leaf 1 — local); the ledger note carries each entry's invocation with paths named `@<world id>/…`, or — when the entry touched a sensitive file — only `{ verb, args = reference, sensitive_files = n }`; `write_note` refuses a note `leaks` flags (class `decision`, nothing written). CART-1192 / CART-1193 |\n| Merge ANOTHER checkout's work into this one — without a line or syntax merge (their intents, replayed here) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/replay.lua <into dir> <from repo> <range> [apply=1] [approvals=<dir>]`, or OVER A SHARED REMOTE `tools/replay.lua <into dir> --remote <name> <range>` (the teammate pushed their branch AND `refs/notes/cartograph`; `replay.fetch` brings the notes into a MIRROR, `refs/notes/cartograph-remotes/<name>`, never merged into this repo's own; no git merge happens — their intents are replayed in your working tree) — reads the INVOCATIONS the ledger notes of `<range>` carry (journal entries record `{ verb, args }` since CART-1191 leaf 1) and re-runs them here in order through the tactic runner; each step classifies ITSELF by its verb's goal check: `done` (already here — the same change on both sides), `applied`, `conflict` (the verb refused it STALE: this world moved at the site — a decision, keep-mine / take-theirs), `frontier` (it did not travel: a sensitive step's args are a hash, its paths name another world, or the entry recorded no invocation). PAST A STOP a later step still runs when it shares NO touched file with any stopped step (the invocation records the plan's touched set; measured: a conflict left 92.3% of later steps independent); a step that shares one — or whose files are unknown (an older entry, a sensitive or cross-world one) — is `not reached` and blocks its own files in turn; `stop_at_first` restores stopping at the first stop. The note carries a REPLAY list: every applied step of the commit's window that touched a file it changed, superseded ones included (a step whose lines a later step overwrote explains nothing, yet the later one was planned against it). Disjoint work commutes (A+replay(B) = B+replay(A), pinned). A preview plans each step against the world AS IT IS; apply=1 is the merge (journaled, undoable). In Lua `require('cartograph.replay')` — `from_notes(repo, range)`, `run(store, items, opts)`, `localize(args, root)`. ⚠ An entry the attribution credits to several commits is replayed once (CART-1195). CART-1191 |\n| How DEPENDENT are replayed steps on each other — would a conflict block work that does not depend on it? | `nvim --