gh it today: behaviour_suppliers, tactic_overrides, `exclude` (per directory walked), `entrypoints` (a real disagreement classifies the file `ambiguous`, never `orphan`). CART-1120 / CART-1160 step 6 |\n| Work on a tree whose full graph does not fit (or need not): hold only a SKELETON and fetch detail on demand, bounded | Ingest the thin index (`ts.index_only(root)`): `store.ingest` installs GENERATED ADAPTERS (`cartograph.adapters`) — reading a detail field (`n.df`, `n.flow`, so `df.stmts(n)`) fills that node's FILE on demand instead of answering nil/{}. The adapters are DERIVED: `adapters.derive()` probes each producer on a tiny skeleton, records the fields it wrote and whether it stayed LOCAL (no graph-level change); only local producers (dataflow) trigger on a read, a non-local one (the call relink) runs only via `adapters.prefetch(store, files, fields)`, which picks the cheapest producer per field. `adapters.budget = N` bounds resident files (LRU eviction; re-read re-fills); `adapters.resident(store)`, `adapters.stats`. Per graph (`data._adapters`); a node fills only while its graph is the lens. Measured (`nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/skeletonrss.lua [root] [budget]`): lua/cartograph, 302 files / 5691 fns — full 70.0 MB heap vs skeleton + adapters 12.3 MB at budget 8, 0 rows differ. ⚠ a presence probe must use `rawget` on a skeleton (a plain read fills). CART-1160 step 8 |\n| What would the graph look like AFTER these edits — without writing them? (an overlay world) | `require('cartograph.world').edit(base_data, { [rel] = text })` -> a VIRTUAL graph: a shallow copy of the base with every node/edge/call record copied, an `overlay` transport layer serving the edited texts first, spliced on the copy (edges INTO an edited file from unchanged callers re-derived). Query it with `store.scoped(over, fn)`; edits STACK (an edit on an overlay keeps the lower texts). The base graph and the disk are untouched (world_spec fingerprints the whole base); planners read the overlay through `txn.read_file`, and `store.content` / `store.stale` / the frontier-text read go through the graph's transport too (an overlay's lines, readfile's semantics); a dry `tactic.run` / `compose.run` chains these worlds step by step; `txn.apply` REFUSES a virtual graph. Acceptance: the overlay equals a rebuild of the edited world, row by row. Transport kind: `{ kind = 'overlay', files = { [abs] = text } }`. CART-1160 step 3 |\n| Run something against a SCRATCH graph without losing the one I have loaded (an example, a fixture, a probe) | `require('cartograph.store').scoped(extraction, fn)` — fn runs with the scratch graph as the lens; the caller's graph (data, generation, range columns) comes back afterwards, raise or not; nothing is re-extracted. Toolbelt examples always run this way. Anything graph-derived held OUTSIDE the store's fields registers in `store.LENS_HOOKS` (fn(data) after every restore) — `at`'s coordinate columns are the first (CART-1159). The arc: CART-1160 (mounts for graphs) |\n| Is there a named tactic for this? Re-measure a recorded discovery — does the finding still hold? | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua list` (every entry, with its examples as usage) · `run <name> <dir> [key=value ...]` (a discovery re-measures the tree; a write tactic PREVIEWS, `apply=1` writes) · `examples [name]` (run them: usage AND test). In process: `require('cartograph.toolbelt').run(store, name, params, opts)`. MCP: `toolbelt_list`, `toolbelt_run { name, params, apply? }` (a write PREVIEWS unless `apply` on a writable host — the whole tactic, `preview` = a diff per file, `worlds`). COMPOSE by name inside a tactic: `T.use(name, params)` — a write entry runs its term, a discovery is a PREMISE gate (fails ill-posed when its claim does not hold); a cycle of uses refuses. Params are coerced by the entry's declaration everywhere (`toolbelt.coerce`): a ref may be `file::name` (did-you-mean on a miss), a string `@path`, a list `a,b`; an undeclared or mi